<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      The one entitlement this product cannot ship without.

      The host dlopens a PKCS #11 module it did not build and Apple did not
      sign — SecureStore's libICASecureStorePkcs11.dylib, or whatever module a
      different card ships. Under the hardened runtime, which notarization
      requires, a process refuses to map any library not signed by the same
      team unless this is set.

      This is exactly what killed Fortify: macOS terminated it a minute or so
      after launch, and the app that replaced it exists because of that failure
      (see docs/pdf-signing-v2.md in the app repository). Removing this line
      produces a build that installs, launches, answers `hello`, and then dies
      the moment anyone asks it to look at a card.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
